When updating policy labels on a namespace, existing pods are evaluated to see if they are compatible with the new policy.

If there are too many pods, or if evaluation takes too long, we short circuit and warn that not all pods were checked.

For that reason, we want to prioritize checking a variety of pods before checking essentially identical pods, so that unique warnings can be surfaced before reaching the pod count cutoff or evaluation timeout.

Pods created by the same controller are most likely to be identical (e.g. replicas from a replicaset or job, copies of pods from a statefulset or daemonset, etc)

```
evaluatedControllers := map[metav1.OwnerReference]bool{}
evaluatedPods := 0
skippedPods := sets.NewInt()
pods := ... get pods in the namespace
for i, pod := range pods {
  if controller, hasController := (... find metadata.ownerReference with controller=true); hasController {
    if evaluatedControllers[controller] {
      // we've already evaluated a pod for this controller... come back to it if we have time
      skippedPods.Insert(i)
      continue
    }
    evaluatedControllers[controller] = true
  }
  // evaluate, short-circuiting and warning on timeout or exceeding max pod count
  ... := evaluatePod(ctx, pod, ...)
}

// if we made it through all unique or controllerless pods, go back over the ones we skipped
for i := range skippedPods {
  // evaluate, short-circuiting and warning on timeout or exceeding max pod count
  ... := evaluatePod(ctx, pods[i], ...)
}
```

